1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,393
There's one chart that

2
00:00:01,393 --> 00:00:02,785
has come before almost

3
00:00:02,785 --> 00:00:04,178
every US recession for

4
00:00:04,178 --> 00:00:05,570
decades — and it's

5
00:00:05,570 --> 00:00:06,963
not about stocks at

6
00:00:06,963 --> 00:00:07,311
all.

7
00:00:07,311 --> 00:00:08,765
It's about government bonds.

8
00:00:08,765 --> 00:00:10,218
Normally, lending money for

9
00:00:10,218 --> 00:00:11,672
longer pays a higher

10
00:00:11,672 --> 00:00:13,125
interest rate than lending

11
00:00:13,125 --> 00:00:14,579
it for a few

12
00:00:14,579 --> 00:00:14,942
months.

13
00:00:14,942 --> 00:00:16,462
That makes sense: more

14
00:00:16,462 --> 00:00:17,982
time means more risk,

15
00:00:17,982 --> 00:00:19,503
so long-term bonds should

16
00:00:19,503 --> 00:00:21,023
pay more. That's the

17
00:00:21,023 --> 00:00:21,783
normal curve.

18
00:00:21,783 --> 00:00:23,049
But sometimes short-term

19
00:00:23,049 --> 00:00:23,471
rates

20
00:00:23,471 --> 00:00:25,158
rise above long-term rates.

21
00:00:25,158 --> 00:00:26,846
That's called an inversion,

22
00:00:26,846 --> 00:00:28,112
and it's rare.

23
00:00:28,112 --> 00:00:29,495
It happens when investors

24
00:00:29,495 --> 00:00:30,878
expect the Fed will

25
00:00:30,878 --> 00:00:32,261
have to cut rates

26
00:00:32,261 --> 00:00:33,644
later, because growth is

27
00:00:33,644 --> 00:00:34,335
slowing down.

28
00:00:34,335 --> 00:00:35,918
It's not a perfect

29
00:00:35,918 --> 00:00:37,501
predictor, and the timing

30
00:00:37,501 --> 00:00:38,689
varies. But historically,

31
00:00:38,689 --> 00:00:39,084
when

32
00:00:39,084 --> 00:00:40,668
this curve flips, a

33
00:00:40,668 --> 00:00:41,855
recession has usually

34
00:00:41,855 --> 00:00:42,251
followed.

35
00:00:42,251 --> 00:00:43,438
Follow for more.
